WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.120
So I want to tell you about a letter

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:06.240
I got a little while ago that I only

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.010
just got around to reading.

00:00:13.759 --> 00:00:17.199
Actually it was in 2003 that it arrived

00:00:17.199 --> 00:00:19.680
but I only just found it on the floor; my

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:21.840
study is very messy.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:24.960
So the author is a contributor to the

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:26.000
OEIS,

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:27.840
a former contributor, I have-

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:29.119
think I have to say,

00:00:29.119 --> 00:00:32.800
called Claude Lenormand. He suggested two

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:34.719
transformations of sequences;

00:00:34.719 --> 00:00:36.880
his second transformation that

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:38.160
introduced a word

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:41.360
I didn't know - a French word - called

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:44.719
a rabot. This is a rabot,

00:00:44.719 --> 00:00:47.039
a plan- a carpenter's plane. And his

00:00:47.039 --> 00:00:48.239
transformation

00:00:48.239 --> 00:00:52.320
is to take a sequence or a word,

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:55.360
a string of numbers, and plane them

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:56.960
down,

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:00.719
raboter, planing the sequence.

00:01:00.719 --> 00:01:03.280
What you plane down are the runs in a

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:04.159
sequence.

00:01:04.159 --> 00:01:05.920
If we have a sequence you might be

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:08.479
tossing a coin and you might get heads,

00:01:08.479 --> 00:01:11.760
heads, tails, tails, tails, heads,

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:14.799
tails. You look at the runs - here we've

00:01:14.799 --> 00:01:16.400
got a run of two heads,

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:18.400
so we get a two. We write down the length

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:19.600
of the runs -

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:23.520
3, 1, 1 - so the runs transformation

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:26.640
of a string, a sequence, is the lengths of

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:27.680
the runs,

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:30.000
that's the runs transformation. What the

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.280
the plane does

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:34.079
it shortens all the runs by one, by one

00:01:34.079 --> 00:01:34.880
turn.

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:37.439
So if we had a run of three things, in

00:01:37.439 --> 00:01:38.400
the transform

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.640
thing we've got a run of two things so

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:42.479
we shorten all the runs,

00:01:42.479 --> 00:01:45.600
that's the operation of raboter. So you

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:46.880
remember in one of the

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:49.680
earlier Numberphile videos you did, you

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:50.560
mentioned

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:54.159
a sequence of my old friend Sol Golomb.

00:01:54.159 --> 00:01:57.200
And this is distinguished by the fact that

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:01.360
it's a sequence of numbers. It's the-

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:04.159
has the property that there- it's an

00:02:04.159 --> 00:02:06.320
increasing, I should say non-decreasing,

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:07.600
sequence, it keeps

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:10.640
growing and it has the property

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:13.040
that if you look at the runs length you

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:14.400
get the same sequence.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.000
All right I'll just do a couple of terms:

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:17.680
1, 2,

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:21.520
2 - how do we continue? Golomb sequence,

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:24.800
the 1 means the first run has length 1.

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:26.879
So we start off with a run of length 1,

00:02:26.879 --> 00:02:28.239
and then we've got a run

00:02:28.239 --> 00:02:29.840
of some other numbers. And you always

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:31.440
pick the smallest number

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:34.879
so the next number has to be a 2. That 2

00:02:34.879 --> 00:02:38.879
says the second run has length 2

00:02:38.879 --> 00:02:43.200
so we've got two 2s. And now that 2,

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:46.400
which is the third term,
says the third run

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:49.680
has length 2. So so yeah so it

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:51.280
continues 3, 3,

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:54.319
and then 4, 4, 4, 5, 5, 5 -

00:02:54.319 --> 00:02:56.480
this will probably make it clearer.

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:59.200
So this 2 refers to those two. The

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:00.000
third term

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.360
is 2 and that says the third run

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:06.480
of successive identica
l terms has length 2.

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:08.879
Well okay we've got our first run, our

00:03:08.879 --> 00:03:09.840
second run,

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:12.400
our third run has to have length 2. So

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:14.400
that means we've got two 3s.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:16.640
The fourth term, and that says the next

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:18.400
run has length 3.

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:20.640
So there are going to be three 4s,

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:21.760
that 3 says

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:25.280
three 4s. This 3 says, aha,

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:27.519
three 5s.
- (Brady: So there'll be four 6s?)

00:03:27.519 --> 00:03:28.640
There are gonna be four

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:30.799
6s! Very good. And there are going to

00:03:30.799 --> 00:03:32.000
be four 7s

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:34.720
from that 4; and that 4 says there

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:36.400
are going to be four 8s,

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:38.080
and that 5 says there are going to be

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:40.319
five 9s.

00:03:40.319 --> 00:03:42.159
That's the defining property. It's

00:03:42.159 --> 00:03:43.360
non-decreasing,

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:45.519
you always put down the smallest number

00:03:45.519 --> 00:03:46.879
you can,

00:03:46.879 --> 00:03:48.879
and it must be its own runs

00:03:48.879 --> 00:03:51.280
transformation. 
So that's Golomb's sequence.

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:54.959
Now if we apply Le Rabot to it,

00:03:54.959 --> 00:03:57.280
and we plane it down, what are we going

00:03:57.280 --> 00:03:58.239
to do? Well

00:03:58.239 --> 00:04:00.720
we're going to cross off that 1. We've

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:01.760
got two 2s

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:03.519
so we throw away one of them. We've got

00:04:03.519 --> 00:04:05.599
two 3s we throw away one of them.

00:04:05.599 --> 00:04:07.519
We've got three 4s we throw away one.

00:04:07.519 --> 00:04:08.959
Five; we just

00:04:08.959 --> 00:04:12.319
shorten all the runs - we apply

00:04:12.319 --> 00:04:16.000
the plane, le rabot. And what we get,

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:19.840
the new sequence, is simply

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:23.600
2, 3, 4, 4, 5,

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:26.639
5, 6, 6, 6, 7,

00:04:26.639 --> 00:04:29.120
7, 7 and so on. So this is the

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:30.560
planed down version

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:33.759
of Golomb's sequence. In

00:04:33.759 --> 00:04:36.880
Claude's letter he gave the example of the

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:40.639
Golomb sequence and how to raboter it.

00:04:40.639 --> 00:04:42.000
So I thought it would be very

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:44.080
interesting to apply

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:47.919
the plane, le rabot, to the numbers

00:04:47.919 --> 00:04:50.080
to the ordinary numbers written in

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:50.960
binary.

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:53.520
Now remember the rule is when you plane

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:54.320
it down,

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:56.160
if it's got a run of length 1 it goes

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:57.680
away. So zero,

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:00.720
well, becomes zero, so we get zero.

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:03.919
1 goes away and we get zero.

00:05:03.919 --> 00:05:05.840
1 goes away, zero goes away, and we get

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:07.120
zero. 11

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:10.560
becomes 1 in base

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:13.360
2, which is 1 in base 10. So our new

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:14.320
sequence

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:15.919
is what you get when you plane down the

00:05:15.919 --> 00:05:18.400
numbers. So we get 000,

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:20.320
this is the base 2 version, this is the

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:21.440
base 10 version.

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:24.720
So 1 0 0 we lose the

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:27.360
1 and we shorten that zero and we get

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:28.960
zero.

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:32.240
1 0 1 - zero, base two is zero base

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:33.030
ten is zero.

00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:34.479
Here now finally we get something

00:05:34.479 --> 00:05:36.240
interesting; we lose the zero

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:39.360
and we lose a 1 so we get 1.

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:43.520
Here 1 1 1, we lose the 1, we got 1 1,

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:47.360
which is 1 1 which is 3. So the sequence

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:51.600
that we're going to
get goes 0 0 0 1 0 0 1 3.

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:54.560
This one we lose that

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:57.919
and that and we get zero; this we lose

00:05:57.919 --> 00:05:58.880
the 1

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:02.319
and that and we get zero; and 10

00:06:02.319 --> 00:06:05.360
we lose everything and

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:08.639
11 we lose that that and that and we

00:06:08.639 --> 00:06:09.840
get 1.

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:12.800
12 we lose a 1 and a zero and

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:13.919
finally

00:06:13.919 --> 00:06:17.360
we get a 2. We're doing 13, we lose

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:20.560
that and that and that and we get a 1.

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:23.600
Because 12 is 1 1 0 0 it

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:24.720
becomes

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:28.080
1 0 which is 2. 13 which is

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:29.199
1 1 0 1

00:06:29.199 --> 00:06:31.280
we lose the 1 the zero and one of the

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:33.280
1s and all we are left with

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:37.039
is a 1. When we get to 14

00:06:37.039 --> 00:06:40.400
we lose a zero and a 1 and we're left

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:41.600
with 1 1 which

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:45.840
is 3. And 1 1 1 1, which is 15,

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:49.039
we lose the 1, we get 1 1 1, and

00:06:49.039 --> 00:06:53.440
we get 7. And when we get down to 16

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:56.800
we get zero again. So that's- and we keep

00:06:56.800 --> 00:06:57.759
going forever.

00:06:57.759 --> 00:07:00.799
So this is the planed down version.

00:07:01.919 --> 00:07:04.560
This has a very nice sound, you know you

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:05.919
can listen to any

00:07:05.919 --> 00:07:08.960
any of the 300,000 sequences in the OEIS

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:12.639
and I would like to play this sequence

00:07:12.639 --> 00:07:13.930
for you:

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:28.960
(How have you turned it into music?) 
- We-

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:30.560
imagine that we have a grand piano,

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:32.479
that's the basic thing, and we number the

00:07:32.479 --> 00:07:36.160
keys 0, 1, 2, 3, up to 87 -

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:39.680
it's got 88 keys. We take the number

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:42.160
and we divide by 88 and keep the

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:43.120
remainder, we

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:46.639
reduce it mod 88. So that gives us

00:07:46.639 --> 00:07:48.639
something we can play on the grand piano.

00:07:48.639 --> 00:07:49.759
So if they're-

00:07:49.759 --> 00:07:51.120
these numbers you can see are all

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:53.520
pretty small so it stays down

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:56.240
here at the bottom of the keyboard for

00:07:56.240 --> 00:07:58.160
quite a long time until we get up to

00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:00.639
some very big numbers, before we actually

00:08:00.639 --> 00:08:02.640
have to round them down.

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:05.759
(Why do you like that piece of music?)

00:08:05.759 --> 00:08:07.120
Why do I like it? Well

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:09.759
it has that rhythm to it, it goes da da

00:08:09.759 --> 00:08:12.400
da da da da

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:18.400
and those those repetitions are because

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:20.160
of the binary expansion.

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:22.879
And then every so often like at

00:08:22.879 --> 00:08:25.440
powers of 2 minus 1 
we get a bigger number.

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:27.440
And before it we get some other big

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:29.280
numbers, so there's a rhythm.

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:33.440
So the sound, the sounds,

00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:37.039
reflect the structure of the binary

00:08:37.039 --> 00:08:37.839
numbers;

00:08:37.839 --> 00:08:40.479
but processed, it's not just the straight

00:08:40.479 --> 00:08:41.650
binary numbers.

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:55.600
(Neil, as you keep planing the binaries,)

00:08:55.600 --> 00:08:57.279
(obviously we were we were staying pretty)

00:08:57.279 --> 00:08:58.720
(low there with those numbers,)

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:00.240
(do you start eventually getting some big)

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:01.760
(numbers when you
turn them into, you know,)

00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:02.480
(base 10?)

00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:05.519
Yeah certainly. When we got up to say 2

00:09:05.519 --> 00:09:07.760
to the 20 minus 1,

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:12.080
plane off 1 we get nineteen 1s in a row,

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:15.440
so eventually we get all - does every

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:16.880
number appear? Yeah sure.

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:19.440
Okay so there's an inverse operation

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:21.920
which is extending all the runs by 1

00:09:21.920 --> 00:09:23.040
bit

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:24.820
and that works for any number.

00:09:24.820 --> 00:09:27.120
(What's that called? If that's- 
if that's le raboter)

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:29.200
what do you call the other version?)
- I

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:32.399
just called it expanding.

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:36.080
Claude didn't mention it in his letter,

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:37.680
but obviously you could do it.

00:09:37.680 --> 00:09:39.760
You expand all the runs by 1. And then

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:41.440
if you applied his operation

00:09:41.440 --> 00:09:43.279
you'd get back to where you started, so

00:09:43.279 --> 00:09:45.279
you can get every number.

00:09:45.279 --> 00:09:47.519
So a question is, after you've planed

00:09:47.519 --> 00:09:48.399
down a number

00:09:48.399 --> 00:09:51.920
how big is it? What's the average value

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:53.920
after you plane it down? So I looked at

00:09:53.920 --> 00:09:56.560
that and

00:09:56.560 --> 00:09:59.519
I asked some friends for help and they

00:09:59.519 --> 00:10:01.440
proved my conjecture is correct.

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:04.480
If n is k bits

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:07.440
long in binary, in other words if it's

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:08.480
a k-bit number.

00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:10.320
So for instance here are all the three

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:12.959
bit numbers:

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:16.560
there are actually four of them. A three

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:17.519
bit number,

00:10:17.519 --> 00:10:19.920
beginning with 1 of course, apart from

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:20.640
zero every

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:22.959
binary number begins with 1. So we

00:10:22.959 --> 00:10:24.800
could have 1 0 0 all the way

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:26.240
up to 1 1 1.

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:29.839
So there are um 2 to the k minus 1

00:10:29.839 --> 00:10:31.440
three bit numbers, there are two squared

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:32.720
numbers, there are four of them. What's

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:33.839
the average value?

00:10:33.839 --> 00:10:36.240
Well it happens to be, we had four

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:37.200
numbers

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:39.760
whose sum is four so the average

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:41.839
value afterwards is 1.

00:10:41.839 --> 00:10:44.160
So what happens for a k-bit number? I

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:45.200
conjectured

00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:48.240
that the average value after is

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:51.680
exactly three halves to the power k minus

00:10:51.680 --> 00:10:54.079
1 minus a half. And the way I found

00:10:54.079 --> 00:10:56.480
that was I worked out the average,

00:10:56.480 --> 00:10:57.839
you can see I just worked out the

00:10:57.839 --> 00:10:59.519
average for 3-bit numbers and I got

00:10:59.519 --> 00:11:01.600
1. If you do that for the first 20 or

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:02.560
30

00:11:02.560 --> 00:11:04.720
terms you get a sequence. What you do

00:11:04.720 --> 00:11:06.160
when you get a sequence? You look it up

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:07.279
in the OEIS.

00:11:07.279 --> 00:11:12.079
And it is sequence A027649,

00:11:12.079 --> 00:11:14.800
which is the integer three halves to the

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:16.880
k minus 1 minus a half.

00:11:16.880 --> 00:11:19.120
So the answer to this question, how big

00:11:19.120 --> 00:11:20.399
is what you get,

00:11:20.399 --> 00:11:23.600
it's about three halves to the k.

00:11:23.600 --> 00:11:26.399
So n was about 2 to the k and we

00:11:26.399 --> 00:11:27.279
shrunk it down

00:11:27.279 --> 00:11:29.440
to three halves to the k, so that's the

00:11:29.440 --> 00:11:30.959
answer.

00:11:30.959 --> 00:11:33.360
You might remember these pieces of pi

00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:35.120
we shared with Numberphile 
supporters a

00:11:35.120 --> 00:11:35.920
while back?

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:37.680
Well they were so popular we've now

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:40.160
created these unique views

00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:43.040
of our beloved Mandelbrot set. Each piece

00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:44.880
is individually numbered and is a

00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:46.160
one-off perspective;

00:11:46.160 --> 00:11:47.839
a different location, a different

00:11:47.839 --> 00:11:50.720
magnification. If you'd like us 
to send you one,

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:54.839
find out more in the link we've included

00:11:54.839 --> 00:11:56.639
below.

00:11:56.639 --> 00:11:58.800
I want to talk about approximating pi in

00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:00.079
the Mandelbrot set,

00:12:00.079 --> 00:12:01.519
maybe it's best to do a little bit of a

00:12:01.519 --> 00:12:03.680
reminder about what 
the Mandelbrot set is?

00:12:03.680 --> 00:12:05.920
So remember the Mandelbrot set was this

00:12:05.920 --> 00:12:08.000
object that we have in the complex plane;

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:11.279
